Adds a streaming world loader. Lots of discussion following :)

### Problem

There is a general inefficiency with loading Polar worlds currently, say you load a world similar to the following:

```java
var instance = new InstanceContainer(UUID.randomUUID(), DimensionType.OVERWORLD);

var mapData = Files.readAllBytes(WORLD_FILE);
var loader = new PolarLoader(PolarReader.read(mapData));
instance.setChunkLoader(loader);

loader.loadInstance(instance);

var loadingChunks = new ArrayList<CompletableFuture<Chunk>>();
loader.world().chunks().forEach(chunk -> loadingChunks.add(instance.loadChunk(chunk.x(), chunk.z())));
CompletableFuture.allOf(loadingChunks.toArray(CompletableFuture[]::new)).join();
```

What you may not initially consider here is that you end up storing multiple copies of the world data.
1. `mapData` containing the compressed raw data
2. `loader.world` has the `PolarWorld` instance which has java representations of the loaded world. Most notably, this contains unpacked block data (eg `int[4096]`s) for each section (!).
3. The Minestom `Chunk` objects (really most of the data is in `Section`s).

You could improve this long term by replacing the chunk loader with a different one, eg `instance.setChunkLoader(IChunkLoader.noop())` at the end. This improves the situation long term (we only store the copy of the world in the Minestom chunk objects). However, this solution still pays for the big memory spike while actually loading the world. If that all seems unnecessary, I agree! It also creates a non-zero amount of extra work to conform with existing APIs, for example we read palette data, expand it to use Palette#setAll, which then packs it back into a long array (there might be a larger discussion around Minestoms API here, but that is ignored for now).

### Presented Solution
The alternative approach given in this PR is to skip the intermediate PolarWorld struct entirely and create Minestom objects directly as we process the world file. This knocks the allocations down to simply the decompressed world data and the cost to instantiate all of the relevant Minestom objects (plus a little more for intermediate state.

Based on my _extremely_ rough experimentation, the streaming loader is ~10x faster and allocates ~30% of the memory when compared to the above example. You should do your own benchmarking depending in your target environment.

Optimizations requiring format changes (not sure if any are worth):
- Store the palette count to avoid having to compute it on load.
- Store the block state IDs for the current version allowing us to skip string parsing when the world is already on the current version (this would notably increase world sizes).

